rulings: ['[{\'date\': \'2018-01-19\', \'text\': "A combined creature is one of the following: a creature put onto the battlefield using Grusilda\'s activated ability, a host creature with an augment card attached to it, a melded creature, or a merged permanent."}, {\'date\': \'2018-01-19\', \'text\': "If you use Grusilda\'s ability to combine a creature card with augment with a nonhost creature card, the resulting creature will have augment but won\'t be attached to a host. It (meaning both cards) will be put into your graveyard as a state-based action."}, {\'date\': \'2018-01-19\', \'text\': "If you use Grusilda\'s ability to combine two host creature cards, you can later attach a card with augment to that creature."}, {\'date\': \'2018-01-19\', \'text\': "You can use Grusilda\'s ability to put a host creature card and a creature card with augment onto the battlefield combined."}]', '[{\'date\': \'2018-01-19\', \'text\': "Any Contraption that\'s on the battlefield and wasn\'t assembled immediately heads to the scrapyard. However, if it\'s a not an actual Contraption card (like Copy Artifact isn\'t), it goes to your graveyard as normal. Non-Contraption cards can\'t be in the scrapyard."}, {\'date\': \'2018-01-19\', \'text\': \'At the beginning of your upkeep, if you control any Contraptions, move the CRANK! counter to the next sprocket. You may then crank any number of Contraptions on that sprocket, causing their abilities to trigger. Cranking a Contraption is always optional.\'}, {\'date\': \'2018-01-19\', \'text\': \'Contraptions are artifacts. Anything that interacts with artifacts will interact with Contraptions.\'}, {\'date\': \'2018-01-19\', \'text\': "Contraptions aren\'t put into your main deck. They go into a separate deck called the Contraption deck."}, {\'date\': \'2018-01-19\', \'text\': "If a Contraption would leave the battlefield and go to any zone other than exile, it instead goes to the scrapyard, the Contraption deck\'s version of the graveyard. Things that affect the graveyard do not affect the scrapyard. You can exile Contraptions just fine."}, {\'date\': \'2018-01-19\', \'text\': \'If you crank multiple Contraptions, their abilities can be put onto the stack in any order. The ability put onto the stack will resolve first.\'}, {\'date\': \'2018-01-19\', \'text\': \'If you or a permanent you control are instructed to assemble a Contraption, reveal the top card of your Contraption deck. Put it onto the battlefield on one of the three sprockets.\'}, {\'date\': \'2018-01-19\', \'text\': "If you or a permanent you control assembles a Contraption and your Contraption deck is empty, nothing happens. You don\'t lose the game."}, {\'date\': \'2018-01-19\', \'text\': \'In Constructed formats, a Contraption deck must have at least fifteen different Contraption cards and no more than one of each.\'}, {\'date\': \'2018-01-19\', \'text\': "In Limited formats, a Contraption deck may include any number of Contraption cards in your card pool. You don\'t have to include every Contraption card you draft or open in sealed deck. In those formats, your Contraption deck may include duplicates."}, {\'date\': \'2018-01-19\', \'text\': \'In silver-bordered games using Contraptions, you have three sprockets, illustrated on the back of Contraption cards. At the start of the game, put a CRANK! counter on sprocket 3.\'}, {\'date\': \'2018-01-19\', \'text\': \'While Contraptions you control are on the battlefield, the Contraption deck is not, even if you are using it to signify the three sprockets.\'}]', "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': '+1/+1 counters and -1/-1 counters on permanents you control no longer annihilate each other. (704.5q)'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'A creature with toughness 0 or less can still attack and block. It blocks an attacking creature with trample, treat its toughness as 0 when determining how to assign combat damage.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Any ability that says a player wins or loses the game still works.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Auras you control stay on the battlefield if they’re attached to an illegal permanent or if they aren’t attached to a permanent (but should be). They may not do much because there probably won’t be an “enchanted [whatever],” but they’ll stay on the battlefield! (704.5m) Similarly, thanks to an un-documented silver-bordered state-based action no longer applying, your creatures with augment can now survive even if not attached to a host. If this happens, its power and toughness are 0/0. (704.5m)'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Because Rules Lawyer applies only to you and other permanents you control, tokens that leave the battlefield will continue to cease to exist. This is also true for copies of cards that might exist off the battlefield.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Creatures you control shrug off opposing creatures with deathtouch. (704.5h)'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Creatures you control that have been dealt lethal damage remain on the battlefield. (704.5g)'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Creatures you control with toughness of 0 or less remain on the battlefield. (704.5f)'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Equipment and Fortifications you control can stay attached to an illegal permanent. (704.5n)'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Good news for Rasputin Dreamweaver fans: you can ignore abilities of permanents you control that say it can’t have more than a certain number of a certain kind of counter. (704.5r)'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'If you somehow control a creature that’s attached to another permanent, that creature can stay attached. It can attack and block, even if another creature is carrying it. Similarly, if you control a permanent that isn’t an Aura, an Equipment, or a Fortification, it can stay attached to whatever you manage to get it attached to. Strange. (704.5p)'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'If you try to draw from an empty library, nothing happens. (704.5b)'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'In Commander, you’re immune to the “commander damage rule” that says you lose if a single commander deals 21 or more combat damage to you. (704.5u)'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Rules Lawyer’s ability doesn’t apply to itself. It can die for any number of reasons. If you’re at 0 or less life and you lose control of Rules Lawyer, you’ll immediately lose the game.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'While you control Rules Lawyer, the following is true, with the exception of not affecting Rules Lawyer itself (Comprehensive Rules references in parentheses):'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'You can control as many legendary permanents (including planeswalkers) with the same name as you want. It’s like your own personal Mirror Gallery! (704.5j)'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'You can control multiple permanents with the supertype world. So, that opens up some deck ideas, I’d imagine. (704.5k)'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'You could hit up an all-you-can-eat poison counter buffet no problem. (704.5c)'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'You don’t lose the game if you’re at or below 0 life. (704.5a)'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Your Two-Headed Giant team can no longer lose because you have 0 or less life or fifteen or more poison counters. (704.5s and 704.5t)'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Your planeswalkers remain on the battlefield even if they have no loyalty counters. (704.5i)'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'A card sleeve is considered part of the card inside it.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'A single die can touch multiple creatures, including ones with different controllers. It will boost any creature you control and cause Ol’ Buzzbark to deal damage to any creature an opponent controls.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'All dice are rolled at the same time.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Dice rolled for Ol’ Buzzbark can’t be greater than one inch in width. Yes, we’ve seen how rolling millions of dice from orbit will destroy Earth. Please don’t do this. We just bought a house.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Once rolled, no player can interfere with the dice. Players also can’t move any permanents on the battlefield until the dice have come to rest.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'To count for either effect, the die must come to rest touching a creature. Touching it mid-roll doesn’t count if it isn’t touching when the die stops rolling.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'A card’s sleeve is considered part of the card. Touching the sleeve of the enchanted creature will cause you to lose life.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'No player loses life for touching Hazmat Suit (Used). The loss of life is only for touching the enchanted creature.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'You can’t physically force another player to touch a card or touch them with the card. Neither one of these will cause the last ability to trigger.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'A combined host/augment creature will get +2/+2 because it has augment.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'The +2/+2 is permanent. It doesn’t wear off as the turn ends.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'You must be able to target a creature you control and a creature you don’t control to cast Really Epic Punch. Note that you can target any creature you control, even if it isn’t a host or doesn’t have augment. It won’t get +2/+2, but it will still fight.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'A creature is facing left if it’s facing the side of the card where the name normally is.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'If there are multiple figures in the art, the one depicting the creature itself is the one that counts.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'A creature is facing right if it’s facing the side of the card where the mana cost normally is.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'If there are multiple figures in the art, the one depicting the creature itself is the one that counts.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'A creature without a text box can later gain abilities. Those abilities will work.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'If Phoebe has abilities that contradict one another, the most recently added one wins. For example, if Phoebe has the text boxes of both Beast of Burden (power and toughness equal to the number of creatures on the battlefield) and Psychosis Crawler (power and toughness equal to the number of cards in your hand). Phoebe’s power and toughness would be defined by the text box added most recently.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'If Phoebe leaves the battlefield, the stolen text boxes are lost. She doesn’t keep them, and she certainly doesn’t give them back to their original owners.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'If Phoebe steals a creature’s text box, that creature no longer has that text box. Phoebe now has her own text box in addition to the one she stole.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'If Phoebe steals a creature’s text box, that creature will no longer have flavor text and can then block Phoebe.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'If a creature loses its text box, leaves the battlefield, and then returns to the battlefield, it will once again have its text box. This won’t affect Phoebe, who presumably still has the one she stole from the original creature.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'If anything cares about Phoebe’s rules text, flavor text, or watermarks, consider all of her text boxes as if they were one. If anything cares about the rules text, flavor text, or watermarks of a creature whose text box was stolen, the effect sees nothing.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'A full list of cards that have been banned and/or restricted in a Constructed format as of the release of Unstable can be found here: https://magic.wizards.com/en/articles/archive/news/unstable-faqawaslfaqpaftidawabiajtbt-2017-12-06'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'A numeral is any digit, such as 2, 7, or 0. Written number words such as “five” don’t count.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'If a player is destroyed, that player loses the game. There aren’t any ways for a player to regenerate… yet.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Some numbers are made up of multiple numerals. For example, if you cast a spell with the number 12 in its text box, the middle ability will trigger if the doom counter is on 1 or 2.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'To move the doom counter one numeral to the left, move it from 5 to 4, from 4 to 3, from 3 to 2, from 2 to 1, or from 1 to 5, as appropriate.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'A silver-bordered game is one where at least one silver-bordered card was in one of the decks. The silver-bordered cards in a silver-bordered game don’t necessarily need to be visible for the players to gain 1 life for high-fiving you.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Each person who high-fives you counts only once, no matter how many times they high-five you.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'The person timing you should let you (and other players in your game) know when 30 seconds has elapsed. You don’t have to be back in your seat at the end of the 30 seconds.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'You must offer high-fives to other people and they must voluntarily accept for it to count.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'A spell must have the word “artifact” or “Contraption” (or their plurals) in its rules to qualify for the bonus. Flavor text, reminder text, name, and the type line don’t count.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'After The Grand Calcutron leaves the battlefield, players pick their cards up and put them back in their hand. They are no longer public and no longer follow the rules of The Grand Calcutron.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'In Commander games allowing silver-bordered cards, The Grand Calcutron can be your commander.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'When The Grand Calcutron enters the battlefield, all players take their hands and place them face up on the table. They then order them as they choose into a row, called a “program.” The only card they may cast is the first card in their program.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Whenever a card would go to a player’s hand (they drew it, it got bounced, etc.) that player can put the card anywhere in their program they wish. At the beginning of each end step, if you have fewer than five cards in your program, you draw cards until you have five.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Your program is still your hand. If you discard a card, it goes from your program to your graveayard.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'After the subgame ends, all players shuffle their cards from the subgame to reform their main-game libraries, and they return to the main game from the point they left off, with The Countdown Is at One finishing resolving.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'If a player has fewer than seven cards in their main-game library, they’ll lose the subgame as it begins, regardless of how many mulligans that player takes.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'If the subgame is a draw, no player won the subgame, so damage dealt to any player in the main game will be doubled.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'To play a Magic subgame, all players start a new game, using their libraries as their decks. No other cards, tokens, emblems, or anything else from the main game are included in the subgame.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Animate Library can’t enchant your Contraption deck.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'If Animate Library leaves the battlefield, your library goes back to being a normal library.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'If your animated library becomes four libraries because of Split Screen, Animate Library stays attached to one of them of your choice. Only that one is a creature.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'While your library is a creature, it can become enchanted or equipped, it can be tapped and untapped, and it can attack and block like other creatures.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Any Contraption that’s on the battlefield and wasn’t assembled immediately heads to the scrapyard. However, if it’s a not an actual Contraption card (like Copy Artifact isn’t), it goes to your graveyard as normal. Non-Contraption cards can’t be in the scrapyard.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'At the beginning of your upkeep, if you control any Contraptions, move the CRANK! counter to the next sprocket. You may then crank any number of Contraptions on that sprocket, causing their abilities to trigger. Cranking a Contraption is always optional.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Contraptions are artifacts. Anything that interacts with artifacts will interact with Contraptions.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Contraptions aren’t put into your main deck. They go into a separate deck called the Contraption deck.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'If a Contraption would leave the battlefield and go to any zone other than exile, it instead goes to the scrapyard, the Contraption deck’s version of the graveyard. Things that affect the graveyard do not affect the scrapyard. You can exile Contraptions just fine.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'If you crank multiple Contraptions, their abilities can be put onto the stack in any order. The ability put onto the stack will resolve first.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'If you or a permanent you control are instructed to assemble a Contraption, reveal the top card of your Contraption deck. Put it onto the battlefield on one of the three sprockets.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'If you or a permanent you control assembles a Contraption and your Contraption deck is empty, nothing happens. You don’t lose the game.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'In Constructed formats, a Contraption deck must have at least fifteen different Contraption cards and no more than one of each.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'In Limited formats, a Contraption deck may include any number of Contraption cards in your card pool. You don’t have to include every Contraption card you draft or open in sealed deck. In those formats, your Contraption deck may include duplicates.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'In silver-bordered games using Contraptions, you have three sprockets, illustrated on the back of Contraption cards. At the start of the game, put a CRANK! counter on sprocket 3.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'While Contraptions you control are on the battlefield, the Contraption deck is not, even if you are using it to signify the three sprockets.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Any die roll that has a numerical result counts, as long as that result is 4 or greater.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'If you roll multiple dice at the same time, each one that results in a roll of 4 or greater will cause the ability to trigger.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Any die roll with a numerical result will add luck counters to As Luck Would Have It. Rolling the planar die will not cause the second ability to trigger.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'The check to win the game happens only as the second ability resolves. For example, if there are 99 luck counters on As Luck Would Have It and you proliferate to add a counter, you won’t automatically win the game.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Any glasses with lenses will count, prescription or not. This includes sunglasses, but doesn’t include monocles or contacts.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'If Blurry Beeble was put onto the battlefield without being cast, it can’t be blocked.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Players generally won’t have time to put on glasses between the announcement of Blurry Beeble and you paying its costs. They must have had glasses on already in order to later block it.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'The glasses don’t necessarily have to be on a player’s face for that player to be wearing them. Hanging around your neck, up on your head, or sitting on your leg to make some adorable little denim monster all count.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'You can’t remove your opponent’s glasses before casting Blurry Beeble, although you may ask them to remove their glasses. They may fall for it.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Any one human hand can represent only one Homunculus token. If you have two hands, you’ll be limited to two such tokens, unless you use other people’s hands, but…'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': '…those people need to agree to do this. You can get other living people to use their still-very-much-attached hands. Your opponent can agree to help you out, but they’re not required to.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'As you choose attackers or blockers, check whether state-based actions would immediately remove any of them from the battlefield. If so, those creatures can’t attack or block, as appropriate.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Creature cards in graveyards attacking or blocking will cause abilities that trigger whenever a creature attacks or blocks to trigger.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Creature cards in graveyards can attack planeswalkers on the battlefield.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'During combat, creature cards in graveyards can attack and block just as if they were on the battlefield. Attacking creature cards in the graveyard can be blocked only by creature cards in the defending player’s graveyard.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'If Over My Dead Bodies leaves the battlefield in the middle of combat, graveyard creatures go back to being just dead. They’re removed from combat.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'If an attacking or blocking creature card is destroyed by a source without undeathtouch, such as Murder, it stays in the graveyard but is removed from combat.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Other combat-relevant abilities such as flying work on attacking or blocking creature cards in graveyards. An attacking creature card with flying in a graveyard could be blocked only by creature cards with flying or reach in graveyards.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Over My Dead Bodies allows you to attack or block with creature cards in your graveyard. If you do, for the duration of combat, the attacking and blocking creature cards are treated as if they are creature cards on the battlefield which means they can be affected by spells or abilities that would affect creatures on the battlefield. That means, for example, if you have a Lord of Accursed on the battlefield (“Other Zombies you control get +1/+1”), all your attacking and blocking graveyard creatures (which Over My Dead Bodies makes into Zombies) would get +1/+1 while in combat.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'You can target attacking or blocking creature cards in graveyards with spells like Giant Growth.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'At the end of the ten seconds, your library must again be a single face-down pile of cards. If it’s not, you must shuffle your library, even if you weren’t touching it when time expired.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Have another player keep time and announce when the ten seconds are up. If you’re still touching any cards in your library when time is up, you have to shuffle your library, undoing your ordering.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'You can’t change the contents of the library.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Augment can (and usually does) change the name, card types, subtypes, rules text, and power/toughness. The combined creature will have (at least) two artists and may now have multiple colors. Anything covered up in the augment process doesn’t count, so ignore things to the left of the “metal bar” in the art of host creatures.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Augment can’t target creatures that aren’t host creatures.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Augment is an activated ability that you activate from your hand. To do so, reveal the card, choose a target host creature, and pay the augment cost. As this ability resolves, if the card with augment is still in your hand, put it onto the battlefield combined with the host creature.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Creatures with augment don’t have a mana cost and can’t be cast.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'The creature card with augment isn’t put onto the battlefield until the ability resolves. This means if the host is destroyed, the creature with augment stays in your hand. You can’t choose a different host, but you can activate augment again if there’s another host available.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'You can’t activate augment unless there is a host creature on the battlefield. It doesn’t need to be yours. Note though that if you augment another player’s host creature, they control the combined creature.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'You can’t put more than one augment card on a single host creature. Once a host creature is augmented, the host part gets covered up and it’s no longer a host creature.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Beginning phase: You untap permanents you control and your opponent doesn’t. Abilities that trigger at the beginning of your upkeep happen and ones that trigger at the beginning of their upkeep don’t. You draw a card as it’s now your draw step, so your opponent doesn’t.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Combat phase: Just like combat on your turn, you can attack your opponents with your creatures. The opponent you stole the phase from can’t attack with their creatures, but they can block.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Ending phase: Most abilities that trigger during the end step don’t care whose turn it is, but a few do. Effects that last “until end of turn” or “this turn” will expire as normal, as they also don’t care whose turn it is.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Postcombat main phase: Same as precombat main phase.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Precombat main phase: You can cast all types of spells while your opponent can only cast instants and spells with flash.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'To steal a phase means that during that player’s next turn, as the chosen phase begins, you treat it as if it were your turn. Here’s what happens if you steal each phase:'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Cards owned by your opponents still go to the appropriate graveyard.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'If an effect asks how many cards are in your graveyard, use the total number of cards in all your graveyards. However, they are still separate graveyards. If an effect affects a single graveyard, such as shuffling it into its owner’s library, only that one graveyard is shuffled in.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'If more than one player controls a Graveyard Busybody, the one that entered the battlefield most recently “wins.” The controller of that Graveyard Busybody has all the graveyards, and any other player’s Graveyard Busybody is a 0/0 that is put into the graveyard that player used to have as a state-based action.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Only you can use abilities of cards in graveyards. For example, if a spell requires that you exile cards from your graveyard, you can exile cards from any graveyard. Any card that looks at cards in graveyards looks at any or all your graveyards.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Speaking of that, cards in graveyards are shuffled into their owners’ libraries. You won’t put any cards from graveyards you stole, er . . . borrowed into your library.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'During each combat, there’s a time when attackers are declared. Normally, only the active player declares attackers, but Party Crasher doesn’t play by those rules. During the declare attackers step of your opponent’s combat phase, after they’re done declaring their attackers, you may choose to declare Party Crasher as an attacker. After you declare blockers, the defending player can declare blockers for Party Crasher. All combat damage is dealt simultaneously, unless a strike ability (first, double, last, and/or triple) is involved.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'If Party Crasher attacks during an opponent’s turn, that opponent could block it with an attacking creature that is untapped as blockers are declared. That creature will then be attacking and blocking at the same time. If Party Crasher is also untapped as blockers are declared, it can block the attacking creature that blocked it. Even if all this happens, the creatures will only deal combat damage to each other once.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Each keyword can be chosen only once on any given day. And that counts every Modular Monstrosity you play with that day. You also can’t reuse a keyword with a new modifier. For example, if you choose protection from Demons and from Dragons (totally counts), you can’t choose protection from red later that day. Protection has already been used.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'If you haven’t made a valid choice after five seconds, Modular Monstrosity loses all keyword abilities, including ones its picked up in other ways.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'If you make an invalid choice, you can still make a valid choice as long as time hasn’t expired.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'You may choose any keyword ability that appears on any printed Magic creature card. You can’t pick keyword actions like explore or scry. If the keyword has a modifier, such as a mana cost or a number, you must choose a modifier that’s been printed. Sorry, “annihilator 500” fans.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Each letter can be guessed only once.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'If Hangman changes controllers, the person who originally chose the word continues to fulfill their duties as letters or words are guessed. Now that this player doesn’t control Hangman, they will have a much easier time figuring out the word.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'If a guessed letter appears multiple times in the word, you should fill in each instance of the letter in the word.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'The abilities mimic the game of hangman. You tell each player how long the secret word is. You may even draw out blanks as one does in the game of hangman.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'You must choose a word in a language each player understands. If there are multiple such languages, you should specify which one you’re using as you secretly note the word.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'For all intents and purposes, the exchanged card is taking the place of the original card. Whatever its doing, the state it’s in, whatever’s attached to it—all of it stays the same. It’s as if Mary O’Kill or one of the Killbots pulls off a mask to reveal they were actually the other one all along. Diabolical!'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Killbot is a creature type. There are several Killbots in the Unstable set.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'You can activate Mary O’Kill’s ability if it is in your hand.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'For art menace, any living or undead being capable of movement counts as a figure. For example, a Treefolk is a figure but a random tree in the background is not.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'For game purposes, your new teammate becomes the owner of the cards you give them for their library, hand, and permanents. Remember to get your cards back after the game.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'If there’s no one around to make a decision, you can call or text someone if you need to. If there’s a physical action required, such as high-fiving, you’ll need someone there.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'It doesn’t have to be someone who plays Magic. This may be fun.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'You and your new teammate become a Two-Headed Giant team and play under Two-Headed Giant rules from that point forward. Similarly, if you pick up a second teammate, you and your two teammates becomes a Three-Headed Giant team, and so on.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'You can choose any player not currently in your game. This includes people who used to be in your game but left it.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'You can give your new teammate Contraptions you control. You can also give them face down cards from your Contraption deck so they can form their own Contraption deck.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Your new teammate must agree to play with you, although they don’t necessarily need to know how to play.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Your new teammate must be someone not already in your game, although it can be someone who was once in your game but left.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'For most recent cards, looking at the expansion symbol will tell you what you need to know. Any card with a black expansion symbol is treated as a common, including basic lands. Any card with a silver expansion symbol is treated as an uncommon. Any card with a gold expansion symbol is treated as a rare. Any card with a mythic orange expansion symbol is treated as a mythic rare. Any card with a different colored expansion symbol (such as the purple expansion symbols from the Time Spiral timeshifted sheet) is treated as a special rarity. Cards from before Exodus don’t have expansion symbols. The rarities of those cards can be found on their Gatherer entries.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'For the first ability, if you move a counter onto a planeswalker, you may have that counter become a loyalty counter.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'For the third ability, you can’t add another CRANK! counter to your Contraption deck while proliferating. The Contraption deck is neither a permanent nor a player.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'For the second ability, you must choose a card with flavor text. You can’t choose a card with no flavor text and say nothing.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'For the third ability, treat hyphenated words as complete words. For example, if you discard a Five-Alarm Fire, you couldn’t find a Five-Finger Discount.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'For the third ability, you’ll be the owner of the cards put into your hand, your library, and your graveyard this way for the rest of the game. Everyone gets their cards back when the game is over.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'When exchanging libraries, don’t change the order of either of them.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Host creatures each have an ability that triggers when it enters the battlefield. It functions like any other creature.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'This card has received minor errata so you can’t flicker creatures more than once each turn. Oops.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Host creatures each have an ability that triggers when it enters the battlefield. It functions like any other creature.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Hyphenated words count as one word.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'If Do-It-Yourself Seraph has abilities that contradict one another, the most recently added one wins. For example, if Do-It-Yourself Seraph has the text boxes of both Beast of Burden (power and toughness equal to the number of creatures on the battlefield) and Psychosis Crawler (power and toughness equal to the number of cards in your hand). Do-It-Yourself Seraph’s power and toughness would be defined by the text box added most recently.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'If Do-It-Yourself Seraph has the text box of another card, it has all the abilities and flavor text of that card.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'If Do-It-Yourself Seraph leaves the battlefield, it loses any text boxes it gained. If it returns to the battlefield, it will be a new object and won’t have those text boxes, either.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'If anything cares about the text of Do-It-Yourself Seraph, consider all of its text boxes as if they were one.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'If Earl of Squirrel deals damage, you’ll create 1/1 green Squirrel creature tokens at the same time the damage is dealt.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'If you control two Earls of Squirrel (Earl of Squirrels? Earl ofs Squirrel?), each will get +1/+1 from the other. Other Squirrels you control will get +2/+2.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'If Masterful Ninja is destroyed or discarded, it goes to your graveyard. It will no longer be on the battlefield or in your hand.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'If you try to move Masterful Ninja from the battlefield to your hand or vice versa, and it’s in both zones, it will just stay in both zones. For example, if Masterful Ninja is in both zones, casting Unsummon targeting it won’t do anything.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'It’s right there.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Look closer.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Masterful Ninja’s first activated ability doesn’t cause any enters-the-battlefield abilities to trigger. When the turn ends and Masterful Ninja stops being both on the battlefield and in your hand, this won’t cause any dies or leaves-the-battlefield abilities to trigger. If Masterful Ninja enters or leaves the battlefield some other way, those abilities work as normal.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'No, that’s a table.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'If Shellephant has first strike, double strike, or triple strike, you can have it deal combat damage as a 3/3 and then change it to a 1/4 before normal combat damage happens. This will also work if the creature Shellephant is in combat with has last strike.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'If you haven’t activated Shellephant’s ability, its power and toughness are 0/0.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'You can change Shellephant’s creature types at any time and while Shellephant is in any zone. It can be a Turtle, an Elephant, or both. “And/or” isn’t a creature type.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'If a token is put in the Cramped Bunker, the object being used to represent the token is what must get in the bunker. You’re not allowed to change what that object is once Cramped Bunker appears. For example, if you’re using a Zombie token card to represent an Angel token (weird, but sure), you can’t suddenly decide you’d rather use a penny. If you create a new Angel token, you can use a penny.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'You can pick up cards touching Cramped Bunker to tap them if you need to, but then they must end up still in contact with Cramped Bunker and not in contact with any other permanents.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'If an ability comes up that requires a target and there are legal targets available, you must use that ability even if you may not want to. However, the effects are all good for you or bad for your opponent. In some cases, you may not be able to take advantage. For example, if it ends up that creatures you control get +1/+1 until end of turn but you don’t control any creatures, you’re out of luck. But nothing bad will happen.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Players can respond to the activation of any of Urza’s abilities, but not after players know what effect is going to happen.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Urza, Academy Headmaster is just like any other planeswalker, except you’re never quite sure what his abilities are going to do. Urza is very unpredictable. To find out what happens, head to AskUrza.com. There you will find three buttons: +1, -1, and -6. Click the button for the loyalty ability that’s resolving, and follow whatever instructions appear.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Urza’s abilities work a little differently with respect to targeting. Unlike other abilities, you may have to choose the targets for an ability as part of that ability resolving. If the ability that comes up requires a target and there are no legal targets available, click again until that’s not true.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'If another spell or ability instructs you to roll two dice and use the difference, you can use The Big Idea’s second ability to replace one of those die rolls. First, roll two dice and use their sum. That’s the first result. Then roll one die. That’s the second result. Then figure out the difference between those two results.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'If you use the second ability to roll two dice instead of one, any ability that triggers “whenever you roll a die” will trigger twice.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Some cards, like Chittering Doom, care about the results of individual die rolls and not the total if two dice are rolled together. For example, if you use The Big Idea’s second ability to replace one die roll with two and roll two 3s, Chittering Doom’s ability (which triggers whenever you roll 4 or higher on a die) won’t trigger.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'If asked to choose a color in a silver-bordered game, you can choose gold.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'The Dragon token is monocolored. It’s gold.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'If one of your libraries is empty, you won’t lose unless you try to draw from that one. Don’t do that. If all of your libraries are empty, you may not have much choice.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'If you also control Yet Another Aether Vortex, any of the four revealed cards that are permanent cards will also be on the battlefield.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'If you cast Animate Library after Split Screen, Animate Library will be attached to only one of the four libraries. This is true even if you cast them in the other order.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'If you cast a second Split Screen, one of your four libraries will itself become four libraries. You’ll end up with seven libraries. If one Split Screen leaves the battlefield, you’ll shuffle all your libraries together. The other Split Screen will still apply, so you’ll play with the top card of your one library revealed. When that one leaves the battlefield, you’ll shuffle your library.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'If you draw a card, you can draw from any of your four libraries.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'If the CRANK! counter is on Sprocket 1, it moves to Sprocket 2. Sprocket 2 to sprocket 3, and Sprocket 3 to Sprocket 1, as normal.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'To crank a Contraption causes its ability to trigger and go on the stack, similar to what happens at the beginning of your turn normally.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'If the creature leaves the battlefield while in the other game, it returns to its home game in the appropriate zone. For example, if it dies in the other game, it will return to its owner’s graveyard in its home game.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'If the damage from Just Desserts gets redirected to a player, use 3.14.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'One Just Desserts will destroy a creature with toughness 3 or less. Two will destroy a creature with toughness 6 or less. You can take out a creature with 25 toughness with eight of them.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'If the person in control doesn’t know anything about Magic, it’s okay to get someone who isn’t you or the controlled player to advise them. Failing that, advising them to just say “go” is okay.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'If the person you’ve enlisted to control your opponent wanders off or refuses to do anything, assume the controlled player did nothing during their turn and end the turn.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'If there’s no one around to make a decision, you can call or text someone if you need to. If there’s a physical action required, such as high-fiving, you’ll need someone there.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'It doesn’t have to be someone who plays Magic. This may be fun.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Only decisions called for by cards and the game rules are affected. The controlling player can’t make the player they’re controlling concede or perform illegal actions.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'The person you enlist makes all decisions for the target player during that player’s next turn. They choose whether any spells are cast, any creatures attack, and so on.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'You can choose any player not currently in your game. This includes people who used to be in your game but left it.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'If there are no other people around to ask, It That Gets Left Hanging won’t gain haste.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'If there’s no one around to make a decision, you can call or text someone if you need to. If there’s a physical action required, such as high-fiving, you’ll need someone there.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'It doesn’t have to be someone who plays Magic. This may be fun.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'You can choose any player not currently in your game. This includes people who used to be in your game but left it.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'You must clearly ask for the high-five, although this needn’t be verbal. The other person must clearly decline, although this too needn’t be verbal.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'If there’s no one around to make a decision, you can call or text someone if you need to. If there’s a physical action required, such as high-fiving, you’ll need someone there.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'It doesn’t have to be someone who plays Magic. This may be fun.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'You can choose any player not currently in your game. This includes people who used to be in your game but left it.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'If you cast a permanent card put into your hand this way, you’ll control the permanent it becomes. However, Sly Spy doesn’t change who its owner is. It’s put into its owner’s graveyard. If it’s returned to its owner’s hand, it will go to the hand of its actual owner, and so on.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'If you cast an instant or sorcery card put into your hand this way, it’ll be put into its owner’s graveyard as it resolves.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'While the card is in your hand, if it would be shuffled into your library, it’s instead shuffled into its owner’s library.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'If you cast the card put into your hand this way, you’ll control the permanent it becomes. However, Five-Finger Discount doesn’t change who its owner is. If it dies, it’s put into its owner’s graveyard. If it’s returned to its owner’s hand, it will go to the hand of its actual owner, and so on.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'While the card is in your hand, if it would be shuffled into your library, it’s instead shuffled into its owner’s library.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'If you choose Flavorful, you’ll draw a card even if the flavor text doesn’t appear in the text box. Lexivore, for example, is eating its own flavor text.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'You can choose differently for each Ineffable Blessing you control. Each will have the ability associated with the choice you made for that Ineffable Blessing. It won’t have the other ability.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'If you give first strike to a creature with last strike, it’ll deal combat damage twice: once during first-strike combat damage and again during last-strike combat damage. If you give a creature double strike to a creature with last strike, it’ll deal combat damage three times: once during first-strike combat damage, again during normal combat damage, and again during last-strike damage. Essentially, double strike + last strike = triple strike.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Last strike is opposite of first strike. A creature with last strike deals combat damage after when most creatures deal combat damage. A creature with last strike deals combat damage only if it survives both first-strike and normal combat damage.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'In a multiplayer game, you can compliment any or all of your opponents.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'You choose whether or not to compliment as the ability resolves.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'You don’t have to compliment an opponent if you’d rather return a creature you control to its owner’s hand or if you just can’t bring yourself to do it. We’ll assume the former.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Include all words in the name, including lowercase words like “of” or “the.”'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'The name of a creature token is the same as its creature type(s), unless the effect that created it gives it a specific name. For example, a Human Soldier creature token is named “Human Soldier.”'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Indefinite articles such as “a” in “draw a card” aren’t number words and can’t be modified.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'You can change power or toughness. If you change a toughness of 1 to 0, the creature will probably die.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'You can change the number of dice rolled for an effect only if the original instruction included a number word to describe how many dice to roll. You can’t change the “a” in “roll a six-sided die,” but you could change the “two” in “roll two six-sided dice.”'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'You can change the number of sides on a die. If you don’t have any five-sided, seven-sided dice, or d21s handy, you can use other dice to simulate the rolls.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'You can change the numeral inside of a mana symbol, but you can’t modify the cost of a spell in time to pay less for it. It must already be on the stack to be a valid target for More or Less.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'You can turn a 0 into a -1. Sources that would deal 0 or less damage don’t deal any damage, even in silver-bordered games.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'You can’t change any instance of X.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Lost fingers can be used for non-game actions, such as eating, drinking, and . . . um, hygiene.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'The thumb is a finger.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Playing a land won’t cause any of Border Guardian’s abilities to trigger. But for the record, Unstable basic lands don’t have a border.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Protection from die rolls means the equipped creature can’t be dealt damage by, equipped or enchanted by, blocked by, or targeted by anything that allows or requires someone to roll a die. This includes any kind of dice, not just six-sided dice. This also includes anything that allows you to reroll a die.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'The equipped creature won’t have protection from anything that cares about the results of dice without allowing or requiring someone to roll a die, such as Snickering Squirrel.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Steel Squirrel can get bonuses larger than +6/+6 due to modified six-sided die rolls or d20s.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'The die roll caused by Steel Squirrel’s activated ability doesn’t have any effect itself, but other abilities (including Steel Squirrel’s first ability) may care about the roll.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Stumps don’t count. They’re like the absence of trees.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'The tree doesn’t have to be alive, but it must be recognizably tree-shaped. Wooden structures that were once trees don’t count.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'The first ability lets you roll a six-sided die and put that result on Socketed Sprocketer. This basically saves that result for later use. Say you roll a 5. Put the die on Socketed Sprocketer with the 5 face up. Later in the game, when you roll another die, you can choose to use the 5 instead of the result. (You’ll see the result before having to decide.) If you have a 6 installed on Socketed Sprocket, you can just uninstall it to draw a card. If you do this, the 6 is gone and can’t be used to replace a future die roll.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'The name of a creature token is the same as its creature type(s), unless the effect that created it gives it a specific name. For example, a Human Soldier creature token is named “Human Soldier.”'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'There just needs to be an open mouth in the art. It can be in the background. It doesn’t have to be on the depicted creature. It also doesn’t need lips. An open skeleton mouth will do.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'To determine how long a name is, count the characters in that name, ignoring spaces.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'To reassemble a Contraption you control means to move it from the sprocket it’s on to either of your two other sprockets. It doesn’t leave the battlefield.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'To turn over a creature means to physically turn the card over. If the result is a face-down card, it’s a colorless 2/2 creature, the same as one would get from using the morph ability. Turning a face-down card over results in it being turned face up. Any abilities that trigger when it’s turned face up will work. Turning a double-faced card over is the same as transforming it. Any abilities that trigger when you transform it will work. Turning a combined host/augment creature over will result in a big colorless 2/2 creature represented by two cards. Turning a melded creature over will result in the two cards breaking apart and forming two separate creatures, but they’ll probably just get right back together. Turning B.F.M. (Big Furry Monster) over is the same as turning a combined creature over.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Triple strike is all the strikes (for now). A creature with triple strike deals first-strike combat damage, regular combat damage, and last-strike combat damage. Note that it has to survive each time to deal more damage later. For example, if a 3/3 creature with triple strike is blocked by a 3/3 creature with first strike, the two creatures will take each other out during first-strike combat damage. But if left unblocked, that creature will deal a total of 9 damage to the defending player or planeswalker.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'You activate X’s first activated ability while X is on the battlefield under your control. This puts X into an opponent’s hand. That opponent plays with their hand revealed, and they’re not allowed to cast X or activate any of X’s abilities—but you are. You can activate X’s second ability to play one of the cards in that hand.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'You choose which card to play as X’s last ability resolves. Your opponent can respond to you activating the ability to get other cards out of their hand. Whatever’s left when the ability resolves is fair game.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'You don’t own the cards you play this way. An instant or sorcery cast this way will go to its owner’s graveyard. A creature card you cast this way will enter the battlefield under your control, but it will be put into its owner’s graveyard if it dies. The same is true for other permanent cards you cast or lands you play.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'You play the card as X’s last ability resolves, with one exception: You can’t play a land this way unless it’s your turn and you have an available land play. If you’re casting a spell this way, you do so as the ability resolves. Ignore any timing restrictions based on the card’s type. You can cast their sorceries or creatures during their turn, for example.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'You can choose 0 as the value of X. However, you won’t roll any dice as it enters and it will be a 0/0. Unless something else is raising its toughness, it will be put into its owner’s graveyard.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'You can choose any artist who has ever been credited on a Magic card or printed token. Even Mark Rosewater.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'You can ignore all nicknames printed on previous silver-bordered cards.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'You can put any established kind of counter on any permanent using the last ability. If that counter has no mechanical relevance, it’s still put on the creature. For example, you can put a loyalty counter on a creature this way. The loyalty counter won’t do anything, but who knows? The creature might one day become a planeswalker, and then it will. Dream big!'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'You can use any unopened, official Magic booster pack, including promotional booster packs.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'You must put all the creature cards revealed this way onto the battlefield.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'You own creatures put onto the battlefield this way. If they die, they’ll go to your graveyard. They can be returned to your hand, shuffled into your library, and so on.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'ability words are part of the creature’s rules text. any capital letters an ability word has will count.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'all capital letters in the target creature’s rules text count, not just the ones in the first word of each sentence.'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'A hat is a garment worn on the head that’s not part of another garment. Hoodies, for example, are not hats. Items that only cover the face and not the top of the head, such as a face mask, are also not hats.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If the card represents multiple creatures and at least one of them is wearing a hat, the creature will have menace.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If the creature is a hat, it won’t have menace, unless it itself is wearing a hat.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'The creature that the card represents must be wearing a hat to have menace. A background character or a scale bird wearing a hat wouldn’t count. (But scale birds in hats! Adorable!)'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Entirely Normal Armchair can be hidden in plain sight (i.e. just sitting there).'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'You must legally get Entirely Normal Armchair into your hand before you can attempt to sneak it onto the battlefield. For example, you can’t sneak it in from your sideboard or collection during a game.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Your opponent may activate Entirely Normal Armchair’s first activated ability even if they can’t literally see it, provided they can identify where it is. That is, you can’t just put your hand over it and claim that can’t see. Well, you can claim that, and it might be true, but it won’t do what you want it to.'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If Slaying Mantis lands on multiple creatures, it fights each of them one-on-one. For example, Slaying Mantis lands on a 3/3, a 4/4, and a 5/5. Slaying Mantis deals 6 damage to each of them, destroying them all. At the same time, they deal a total of 12 damage to Slaying Mantis, destroying it. What a maneuver!'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If Slaying Mantis touches the object used to represent a creature token an opponent controls, it will fight that creature.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If you create a token that’s a copy of Slaying Mantis, it also enters via flying elbow. However, you must use a Magic card-sized object to represent the token. As Slaying Mantis’s just a second ability won’t apply, players can move around their permanents at any time, including while Slaying Mantis is in flight. So that’s fun.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Just a second is a variant of split second. Announcing Slaying Mantis instantly locks all permanents in place on the battlefield. As long as Slaying Mantis is on the stack, players can’t cast spells, activate abilities, or move their permanents.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'No player may interfere with the throwing of Slaying Mantis, including while it’s in flight.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Slaying Mantis must be thrown onto the battlefield from at least three feet away. That is, three feet is a horizonal distance, not a vertical one.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Slaying Mantis will fight any creature an opponent controls that it touched as it entered, even if it didn’t end up touching that creature when it stopped moving.'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If Super-Duper Death Ray doesn’t resolve because its target leaves the battlefield or otherwise becomes an illegal target in response, it will have no effect. It won’t deal damage to any creature or player.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If damage is already marked on the creature, that damage is considered when calculating excess damage. For example, say your opponent controls a 6/6 creature. Your first Super-Duper Death Ray deals 4 damage to it. Then your SECOND Super-Duper Death Ray (i.e. the one they never see coming) deals 2 damage to the creature and 2 damage to your opponent.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Super-Duper Death Ray targets only the creature. It can deal damage to a player with hexproof, for example.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Trample on an instant or sorcery is similar to how it works on an attacking creature. If Super-Duper Death Ray would deal more damage that what it would take to destroy the creature (called “excess damage”), it deals the excess to the creature’s controller. For example, if you aim Super-Duper Death Ray at a 1/1 creature, it will deal 1 damage to the creature and 3 damage to its controller.'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If a player rolls a die, you can tap an untapped Wall you control to have that player reroll that die. Then, if you didn’t like that result, you can tap another untapped Wall you control in order to have them reroll again.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'The Wall you tap to use Wall of Fortune’s ability can be one that came under your control this turn. This includes Wall of Fortune itself.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'You can use Wall of Fortune to have a player reroll any die, including the planar die or the d20 from Sword of Dungeons & Dragons.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'You decide whether to use Wall of Fortune’s ability right after you see the result of the die roll.'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If an effect sets your life total to a specific number, it will be that number, even if you previously had infinite life.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If you give Infinity Elemental lifelink and it deals damage, you will gain infinite life. You might think infinite life sounds like a lot, and you’d be right. Once at infinite life, your life total mostly can’t change (see below). At infinite life and gain 2 life? You’re at infinite life. At infinite life and lose 29 life? You’re at infinite life. You can pay any amount of life and still be at infinite life. In fact, if you’re at infinite life and get with an opposing Infinity Elemental, you’d still be at infinite life.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If you’re at infinite life and attempt to draw a card from an empty library . . . you lose. You’re not immortal.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Infinity Elemental’s power is incalculable. Infinity Elemental has the greatest power among creatures on the battlefield (and off the battlefield for that matter), although it will tie with other Infinity Elementals. Gaining or losing power is meaningless to it, although effects that set its power to another number will still work.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Through various other cards, you can convert the infinite power of Infinity Elemental into other resources. For example, you could have infinite mana or create infinite tokens. You could draw infinite cards, but that many card don’t exist, so it’s probably not a great idea. Forcing another player to draw infinite cards, on the other hand . . . .'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If the die roll includes a physical component, such as Ol’ Buzzbark caring where the dice land, roll two dice for each one you would roll. You may need to use colored dice to keep each pair of dice clear so you can ignore one roll from each pair.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If you are instructed to roll multiple dice, roll two for each one and choose which one counts.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Krark’s Other Thumb applies to rerolls as well.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Krark’s Other Thumb works with any die you’re instructed or allowed to roll.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'The ignored roll never happened as far as any other effect is concerned. For example, an ability that triggers “whenever you roll a die” won’t trigger for the ignored roll.'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If you are playing with your head on the table, other players can’t attempt to physically dislodge you. They can try and talk you into moving.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If you’re not playing on a table, putting your head on the playing surface will count. (Wait, what are you playing on? Doesn’t matter.)'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'You can use any part of your head, such as your chin.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'You don’t have to put your head on the table if you don’t want to. The enters-the-battlefield ability will still destroy the target creature, and Skull Saucer will then be sacrificed.'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Multiple Snickering Squirrels can be used on the same die roll. In this way, you can produce results that really aren’t normally possible, such as a result of 83 on a six-sided die.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Snickering Squirrel can be used to produce results that aren’t normally possible, such as a result of 7 on a six-sided die.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'You can use Snickering Squirrel’s ability even if Snickering Squirrel came under your control this turn. (Note that it doesn’t use the {T} symbol.)'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'You decide whether to use Snickering Squirrel right after you see the result of the die roll.'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': '“Doubles” means the results of each of the two rolls is identical, after applying any effects that may alter or affect the roll. For example, if you roll a 5 and a 6, and then use Snickering Squirrel to change the 5 into a 6, you’ve rolled doubles.'}]"]
foreign_names: []
printings: ['10E', '2ED', '2XM', '30A', '3ED', '40K', '4BB', '4ED', '5ED', '6ED', '7ED', '8ED', '9ED', 'AFR', 'AKH', 'AKR', 'ALA', 'ANA', 'ANB', 'ARC', 'ARN', 'ATH', 'AVR', 'BBD', 'BFZ', 'BLB', 'BRB', 'BRC', 'BRO', 'BTD', 'C13', 'C14', 'C15', 'C16', 'C17', 'C18', 'C19', 'CED', 'CEI', 'CHK', 'CLB', 'CLU', 'CM2', 'CMA', 'CMD', 'CMM', 'CMR', 'CST', 'DD1', 'DD2', 'DDC', 'DDD', 'DDE', 'DDF', 'DDG', 'DDH', 'DDI', 'DDJ', 'DDK', 'DDL', 'DDM', 'DDN', 'DDO', 'DDP', 'DDQ', 'DDR', 'DDS', 'DDT', 'DDU', 'DKM', 'DMR', 'DMU', 'DOM', 'DPA', 'DTK', 'DVD', 'E01', 'ELD', 'EVG', 'FBB', 'FRF', 'FUT', 'G17', 'GK1', 'GK2', 'GN2', 'GN3', 'GNT', 'GRN', 'GS1', 'GVL', 'H09', 'H17', 'HBG', 'HOP', 'HOU', 'ICE', 'IKO', 'INV', 'ISD', 'ITP', 'J14', 'J17', 'J22', 'JMP', 'JVC', 'KHM', 'KLD', 'KLR', 'KTK', 'LCI', 'LEA', 'LEB', 'LRW', 'LTR', 'M10', 'M11', 'M12', 'M13', 'M14', 'M15', 'M19', 'M20', 'M21', 'MBS', 'MD1', 'ME1', 'ME3', 'MH2', 'MH3', 'MID', 'MIR', 'MKM', 'MMQ', 'MOM', 'MRD', 'NEO', 'NPH', 'ODY', 'OLEP', 'ONE', 'ONS', 'ORI', 'OTJ', 'P02', 'P23', 'PAL00', 'PAL01', 'PAL02', 'PAL03', 'PAL04', 'PAL05', 'PAL06', 'PAL99', 'PALP', 'PANA', 'PARL', 'PC2', 'PCA', 'PD2', 'PD3', 'PDGM', 'PELP', 'PF19', 'PF20', 'PGPX', 'PGRU', 'PHUK', 'PIP', 'PL24', 'PLST', 'PMPS', 'PMPS06', 'PMPS07', 'PMPS08', 'PMPS09', 'PMPS10', 'PMPS11', 'POR', 'PPP1', 'PRM', 'PRW2', 'PRWK', 'PS11', 'PSAL', 'PSS2', 'PSS3', 'PSS4', 'PTC', 'PTK', 'PUST', 'PZ2', 'RAV', 'REX', 'RIX', 'RNA', 'ROE', 'RQS', 'RTR', 'S99', 'SCD', 'SHM', 'SIR', 'SLD', 'SNC', 'SOI', 'SOM', 'STX', 'SUM', 'TD0', 'TD2', 'THB', 'THS', 'TMP', 'TPR', 'TSP', 'UGL', 'ULST', 'UND', 'UNF', 'UNH', 'USG', 'UST', 'VOW', 'WAR', 'WC00', 'WC01', 'WC02', 'WC03', 'WC04', 'WC97', 'WC98', 'WC99', 'WHO', 'WOE', 'XANA', 'XLN', 'ZEN', 'ZNR']
original_text: ['(This creature has INFINITE POWER.)', '({PB} can be paid with either {B} or 2 life.)\\n{PB}{PB}{PB}{PB}: Choose a card you own from outside the game that has been banned or restricted in a Constructed format, reveal that card, and put it into your hand.', '+1: Head to Ask\\nUrza.com and click +1.\\n-1: Head to Ask\\nUrza.com and click -1.\\n-6: Head to Ask\\nUrza.com and click -6.', "A person outside the game becomes your teammate. (Choose any number of cards in your hand, on top of your library, or on the battlefield under your control. Those cards become your teammate's hand, library, and permanents, respectively.)", 'A person outside the game chooses an attacking or blocking creature target opponent controls. That player sacrifices that creature.', "A person outside the game looks at target opponent's hand and chooses a nonland card from it. That player discards that card.", 'Add or subtract 1 or one from a number or number word on target spell or permanent until end of turn.', "All graveyards are also your graveyards.\\nGraveyard Busybody's power and toughness are each equal to the number of cards with flavor text in your graveyards.", 'As Buzzing Whack-a-Doodle enters the battlefield, you and an opponent each secretly choose Whack or Doodle. Then those choices are revealed. If the choices match, Buzzing Whack-a-Doodle has that ability. Otherwise, it has Buzz.\\n• Whack — {T}: Target player loses 2 life.\\n• Doodle — {T}: You gain 3 life.\\n• Buzz — {2}, {T}: Draw a card.', 'As Hammer Jammer enters the battlefield, roll a six-sided die. Hammer Jammer enters the battlefield with a number of +1/+1 counters on it equal to the result.\\nWhenever you roll a die, remove all +1/+1 counters from Hammer Jammer, then put a number of +1/+1 counters on it equal to the result.', "As Hangman enters the battlefield, secretly note a word with six to eight letters.\\n{1}: Target player who doesn't control Hangman guesses the noted word or an unguessed letter in that word. If he or she guesses wrong, put a +1/+1 counter on Hangman. Any player may activate this ability.\\nWhen a player guesses the noted word or all of its letters, sacrifice Hangman.", 'As Hydradoodle enters the battlefield, roll X six-sided dice. Hydradoodle enters the battlefield with a number of +1/+1 counters on it equal to the total of those results.\\nReach, trample', 'As Ineffable Blessing enters the battlefield, choose Flavorful or Bland.\\n• Flavorful — Whenever a creature with flavor text enters the battlefield under your control, draw a card.\\n• Bland — Whenever a creature without flavor text enters the battlefield under your control, draw a card.', 'As Ineffable Blessing enters the battlefield, choose a number.\\nWhenever a creature with exactly the chosen number of words in its name enters the battlefield under your control, draw a card. (Hyphenated words are one word.)', 'As Ineffable Blessing enters the battlefield, choose a rarity.\\nWhenever a creature with the chosen rarity enters the battlefield under your control, draw a card.', 'As Ineffable Blessing enters the battlefield, choose an artist.\\nWhenever a creature with art by the chosen artist enters the battlefield under your control, draw a card.', 'As Ineffable Blessing enters the battlefield, choose odd or even.\\nWhenever a creature with a collector number of the chosen value enters the battlefield under your control, draw a card.', 'As Ineffable Blessing enters the battlefield, choose white-bordered or silver-bordered.\\nWhenever a creature with the chosen border enters the battlefield under your control, draw a card.', "As Staff of the Letter Magus enters the battlefield, choose a consonant other than N, R, S, or T.\\nWhenever a player casts a spell, you gain 1 life for each time the chosen letter appears in that spell's name.", 'As Stamp of Approval enters the battlefield, choose a watermark.\\nCreatures you control with the chosen watermark get +1/+1.', 'As an additional cost to cast Midlife Upgrade, sacrifice X Contraptions.\\nAssemble X plus one Contraptions. (To assemble a Contraption, put the top card of your Contraption deck face up onto one of your sprockets.)', "As long as X is in X's owner's opponent's hand, X's owner may cast X and activate X's abilities. That opponent can't cast X and plays with his or her hand revealed.\\n{U}{B}, {T}: Put X into target opponent's hand.\\n{3}{U}{B}: You may play a card in the same hand as X without paying its mana cost.", 'Assemble X Contraptions. (To assemble a Contraption, put the top card of your Contraption deck face up onto one of your sprockets.)', 'Assemble two Contraptions. (Put the top card of your Contraption deck face up onto one of your sprockets. Then repeat this process.)', 'At the beginning of each end step, if an artifact entered the battlefield under your control this turn,\\nAugment {2} ({2}, Reveal this card from your hand: Combine it with target host. Augment only as a sorcery.)', 'At the beginning of each end step, if you rolled a die this turn,\\nAugment {4}{G} ({4}{G}, Reveal this card from your hand: Combine it with target host. Augment only as a sorcery.)', "At the beginning of each opponent's upkeep, that player moves a permanent he or she controls to touch Cramped Bunker and no other permanents. If he or she can't, destroy each permanent that player controls that isn't touching Cramped Bunker, then sacrifice it.", 'At the beginning of your upkeep, roll a six-sided die. On a 3 or 4, put a +1/+1 counter on Inhumaniac. On a 5 or higher, put two +1/+1 counters on it. On a 1, remove all +1/+1 counters from Inhumaniac.', 'At the beginning of your upkeep, roll a six-sided die. Painiac gets +X/+0 until end of turn, where X is the result.', 'At the beginning of your upkeep,\\nAugment {5}{U} ({5}{U}, Reveal this card from your hand: Combine it with target host. Augment only as a sorcery.)', 'Baron Von Count enters the battlefield with a doom counter on "5."\\nWhenever you cast a spell with the indicated numeral in its mana cost, text box, power, or toughness, move the doom counter one numeral to the left.\\nWhen the doom counter moves from "1," destroy target player and put that doom counter on "5."', 'Battle cry (Whenever this creature attacks, each other attacking creature gets +1/+0 until end of turn.)\\nWhen Garbage Elemental enters the battlefield, roll two six-sided dice. Create a number of 1/1 red Goblin creature tokens equal to the difference between those results.', 'Blurry (This creature can be blocked only if defending player was wearing glasses as it was cast.)\\nWhenever Blurry Beeble deals combat damage to a player, you may draw a card. If you do, discard a card.', 'Cascade (When you cast this spell, exile cards from the top of your library until you exile a nonland card that costs less. You may cast it without paying its mana cost. Put the exiled cards on the bottom of your library in a random order.)\\nWhen Garbage Elemental enters the battlefield, roll a six-sided die. Garbage Elemental deals damage equal to the result to target opponent.', 'Choose one —\\n• Destroy each creature with an odd number of words in its name. (Hyphenated words are one word.)\\n• Destroy each creature with an even number of words in its name.', "Choose two —\\n• Counter target black-bordered spell.\\n• Return target creature to its owner's hand.\\n• Untap each permanent you control with a watermark.\\n• Roll two six-sided dice. Target player puts the top X cards of his or her library into his or her graveyard, where X is the total of those results.", "Choose two —\\n• Draw a card from an opponent's library.\\n• Copy target instant or sorcery spell. You may choose new targets for the copy.\\n• Until end of turn, target creature loses all abilities and becomes a blue Frog with base power and toughness 1/1.\\n• Create a 1/1 colorless Gnome artifact creature token.", "Choose two —\\n• Return target permanent to its controller's hand.\\n• Draw two cards, then discard a card.\\n• Change the target of target spell with a single target.\\n• Turn over target nontoken creature.", "Choose two —\\n• Scry 3.\\n• Create a 2/2 black Rogue creature token with menace.\\n• Add or subtract 1 or one from a number or number word on target spell or permanent until end of turn.\\n• Return all artifacts target player controls to their owner's hand.", "Choose two —\\n• Switch target creature's power and toughness until end of turn.\\n• Target creature can't be blocked this turn.\\n• Draw a card. If that card's art is by Wayne England, you may reveal it and draw another card.\\n• Assemble a Contraption.", 'Choose two —\\n• Untap two target permanents.\\n• Tap each permanent target player controls with exactly one word in its name.\\n• Discard all the cards in your hand, then draw that many cards.\\n• Return target instant or sorcery card from your graveyard to your hand.', 'Combined, enchanted, and equipped creatures you control have menace.\\n{3}{B}{R}, {T}: Put two target creature cards from graveyards onto the battlefield combined into one creature under your control. (Its power is equal to their total power, its toughness is equal to their total toughness, and it has their names, mana costs, types, text boxes, etc.)', 'Counter target spell, then assemble a Contraption. (Put the top card of your Contraption deck face up onto one of your sprockets.)', 'Create a 1/2 blue Bird creature token with flying named Storm Crow.\\nStorm (When you cast this spell, copy it for each spell cast before it this turn.)', 'Creature cards in graveyards can attack and block as though they were on the battlefield, can block or be blocked only by creature cards in graveyards, are Zombies in addition to their other types, and have undeathtouch. (If they would deal damage to a creature card, exile that creature card instead.)\\nCreature cards in your graveyard have haste.', 'Deathtouch\\nWhen Steady-Handed Mook enters the battlefield, it assembles a Contraption. (Put the top card of your Contraption deck face up onto one of your sprockets.)', "Defective Detective can't be blocked.\\nWhen Defective Detective enters the battlefield, a person outside the game looks at target opponent's hand and chooses a card from it. That player reveals that card.", 'Defender\\nYou may tap an untapped Wall you control to have any player reroll a die that player rolled.', 'Destroy target artifact or enchantment. Assemble a Contraption. (Put the top card of your Contraption deck face up onto one of your sprockets.)', 'Destroy target creature, then assemble a Contraption. (Put the top card of your Contraption deck face up onto one of your sprockets.)', "Dr. Julius Jumblemorph is every creature type (even if this card isn't on the battlefield).\\nWhenever a host enters the battlefield under your control, you may search your library and/or graveyard for a card with augment and combine it with that host. If you search your library this way, shuffle it.", "During your turn, if Entirely Normal Armchair is in your hand, you may hide it on the battlefield.\\n{0}: Return Entirely Normal Armchair to its owner's hand. Only any opponent may activate this ability and only if he or she sees Entirely Normal Armchair.\\n{2}, Sacrifice Entirely Normal Armchair: Destroy target attacking creature.", 'Enchant creature\\nAs Magic Word enters the battlefield, choose a word.\\nWhisper the chosen word: Tap enchanted creature.', "Enchant creature\\nEnchanted creature gets +2/+1 and has menace.\\nWhenever a player's skin or fingernail touches enchanted creature, that player loses 2 life.", "Enchant your library\\nEnchanted library is an artifact creature on the battlefield with power and toughness each equal to the number of cards in it. It's still a library.\\nIf enchanted library would leave the battlefield, exile Animate Library instead.", 'Equipped creature gets +2/+1 and has protection from die rolls. (Nothing that lets a player roll a die can block, target, deal damage to, or attach to equipped creature.)\\nEquip {2}', 'Equipped creature gets +2/+2 and has protection from Rogues and from Clerics.\\nWhenever equipped creature deals combat damage to a player, create a 4/4 gold Dragon creature token with flying and roll a d20 (a twenty-sided die). If you roll a 20, repeat this process.\\nEquip {2}', 'Equipped creature has "{T}: This creature deals 1 damage to target player. Roll a six-sided die. On a 5 or higher, untap it."\\nEquip {2}', 'First strike, protection from black borders (Nothing with a black border can block, target, deal damage to, or attach to this creature.)', 'First strike, protection from even collector numbers (Nothing with an even collector number can block, target, deal damage to, or attach to this creature.)', 'First strike, protection from loose lips (Nothing with an open mouth in its artwork can block, target, deal damage to, or attach to this creature.)', 'First strike, protection from odd collector numbers (Nothing with an odd collector number can block, target, deal damage to, or attach to this creature.)', 'First strike, protection from two-word names (Nothing with exactly two words in its name can block, target, deal damage to, or attach to this creature. Hyphenated words are one word.)', 'First strike, protection from watermarks (Nothing with a watermark can block, target, deal damage to, or attach to this creature.)', 'Flying\\nNovellamental can block only creatures with flying.', "Flying\\nOld-Fashioned Vampire gets +2/+2 and has deathtouch as long as it's dark outdoors.", 'Flying\\nWhen Chipper Chopper enters the battlefield, you may sacrifice another artifact. If you do, put two +1/+1 counters on Chipper Chopper and it assembles a Contraption. (Put the top card of your Contraption deck face up onto one of your sprockets.)', "Flying\\nWhen Chivalrous Chevalier enters the battlefield, return a creature you control to its owner's hand unless you compliment an opponent.", 'Flying\\nWhen Skull Saucer enters the battlefield, destroy target creature and put your head on the table. Sacrifice Skull Saucer when your head stops touching the table.', 'Flying\\nWhen this creature enters the battlefield, you may destroy target nonland permanent.', 'Flying\\nWhenever Do-It-Yourself Seraph attacks, you may search your library for an artifact card, exile it, then shuffle your library.\\nDo-It-Yourself Seraph has the text box of each card exiled with Do-It-Yourself Seraph in addition to its own.', "Flying\\nWhenever Spy Eye deals combat damage to a player, you may draw a card from that player's library.", 'Flying\\nWhenever you attack with two or more creatures,\\nAugment {3}{W} ({3}{W}, Reveal this card from your hand: Combine it with target host. Augment only as a sorcery.)', 'Flying\\n{3}{W}, Sacrifice another artifact: Aerial Toastmaster assembles a Contraption. (Put the top card of your Contraption deck face up onto one of your sprockets.)', 'Flying\\n{4}: Reassemble target Contraption you control. (Move it onto another one of your sprockets.)', "Frenzy 2 (Whenever this creature attacks and isn't blocked, it gets +2/+0 until end of turn.)\\nGarbage Elemental can't be blocked by wordy creatures. (A creature is wordy if it has four or more lines of rules text.)", 'Gain control of target creature until end of turn. Untap that creature and roll a six-sided die. Until end of turn, it gains haste and gets +X/+0, where X is the result.', 'Haste\\nReveal Masterful Ninja from your hand: Masterful Ninja is on the battlefield and in your hand until end of turn.\\n{1}{B}: Masterful Ninja gets +1/+1 until end of turn.', "Haste\\nYou can attack with Party Crasher once each combat during each opponent's turn.", 'Hexproof\\nWhenever you roll a die, put a number of luck counters on As Luck Would Have It equal to the result. Then if there are 100 or more luck counters on As Luck Would Have It, you win the game. (Count both rolls if you reroll a die.)', "Hoisted Hireling has flying as long as it's being held above the battlefield.", 'If you would roll a die, instead roll two of those dice and ignore one of those results.', 'Increase the result of each die you roll by 2.', "Just Desserts deals π damage to target creature. (π is the ratio of a circle's circumference to its diameter. (It's a smidgen more than 3.))", "Just a second (As long as this spell is on the stack, players can't move cards on the battlefield.)\\nSlaying Mantis enters the battlefield by being thrown from a distance of at least three feet.\\nWhen Slaying Mantis enters the battlefield, it fights each creature an opponent controls that it touched as it entered.", 'Last strike (This creature deals combat damage after creatures without last strike.)', "Last strike (This creature deals combat damage after creatures without last strike.)\\nBattalion — Whenever Garbage Elemental and at least two other creatures attack, target creature can't block this turn.", "Menace (This creature can't be blocked except by two or more creatures.)\\nOther creatures you control wearing hats in their art have menace.", 'Menace\\nWhenever Overt Operative deals combat damage to a player, it assembles a Contraption. (Put the top card of your Contraption deck face up onto one of your sprockets.)', "Open a sealed Magic booster pack, reveal the cards, and put all creature cards revealed this way onto the battlefield under your control. They're Zombies in addition to their other types. (Remove those cards from your deck before beginning a new game.)", 'Other Riggers you control get +1/+0 and have haste.\\nIf a Rigger you control would assemble a Contraption, it assembles two Contraptions instead.', "Phoebe, Head of S.N.E.A.K. can't be blocked by creatures with flavor text.\\n{2}{U}{B}: Phoebe permanently steals target creature's text box. (That creature loses all rules text, flavor text, and watermarks. This creature gains them.)", "Players play a Magic subgame, starting at 1 life and using their libraries as their decks. For the rest of the main game, if a source would deal damage to a player who didn't win the subgame, it deals double that damage to that player instead.", 'Prowess (Whenever you cast a noncreature spell, this creature gets +1/+1 until end of turn.)', 'Put target nonland permanent into your hand. You may spend mana as though it were mana of any color the next time you cast that card.', 'Reach\\nWhenever you roll a 4 or higher on a die, put a +1/+1 counter on Willing Test Subject.\\n{6}: Roll a six-sided die.', 'Roll a six-sided die. Assemble a number of Contraptions equal to the result. (To assemble a Contraption, put the top card of your Contraption deck face up onto one of your sprockets.)', 'Roll a six-sided die. Create a number of 1/1 red Goblin creature tokens equal to the result.', "Roll a six-sided die. Put target nonland permanent into its owner's library just beneath the top X cards of that library, where X is the result.", 'Sacrifice Amateur Auteur: Destroy target enchantment.', 'Sacrifice a land: You gain 2 life.\\nSacrifice a creature: Add {C}{C} to your mana pool.\\n{2}, Discard a card: Search your library for a card that shares a complete word in its name with the name of the discarded card, reveal it, put it into your hand, then shuffle your library.', "Search your library for a basic land card and reveal it. If there's a tree in its art, put it onto the battlefield tapped. Otherwise, put it into your hand. Then shuffle your library.", 'Search your library for a host card or a card with augment, reveal it, put it into your hand, then shuffle your library.', 'Spells you cast that refer to artifacts or Contraptions in their rules text cost {1} less to cast.', 'Squirrellink (Damage dealt by this creature also causes you to create that many 1/1 green Squirrel creature tokens.)\\nCreature tokens you control are Squirrels in addition to their other creature types.\\nOther Squirrels you control get +1/+1.', "State-based actions don't apply to you or other permanents you control. (You don't lose the game due to having 0 or less life or drawing from an empty library. Your creatures aren't destroyed due to damage or deathtouch and aren't put into a graveyard due to having 0 or less toughness. Your planeswalkers aren't put into a graveyard if they have 0 loyalty. You don't put a legendary permanent into a graveyard if you control two with the same name. Counters aren't removed from your permanents due to game rules. Permanents you control attached or combined illegally remain on the battlefield. For complete rules and regulations, see rule 704.)", "Target creature gets +2/+2 until end of turn. If it's a host or has augment, it gains lifelink until end of turn.", "Target creature you control gets +2/+2 if it's a host or has augment. Then it fights target creature you don't control.", 'Target player in a silver-bordered game you can see from your seat gains control of target creature you control until your next turn. At the beginning of your next upkeep, put two +1/+1 counters on that creature. (A creature is on the battlefield of only the game its controller is playing.)', 'Trample', "Trample (This spell can deal excess damage to its target's controller.)\\nSuper-Duper Death Ray deals 4 damage to target creature.", 'Trample\\nAt the beginning of each end step, if an opponent was dealt damage this turn,\\nAugment {1}{R}{R} ({1}{R}{R}, Reveal this card from your hand: Combine it with target host. Augment only as a sorcery.)', 'Triple strike (This creature deals first-strike, regular, and last-strike combat damage.)', "Unleash (You may have this creature enter the battlefield with a +1/+1 counter on it. It can't block as long as it has a +1/+1 counter on it.)\\nEach creature you control with any kind of counter on it has art menace. (They can't be blocked except by creatures with two or more visible figures in their art.)", "Vigilance\\nKnight of the Widget's power and toughness are each equal to the number of Order of the Widget watermarks among permanents you control.", "When Clocknapper enters the battlefield, choose beginning phase, precombat main phase, combat phase, postcombat main phase, or ending phase. Steal that phase from target player during his or her next turn. (That phase occurs as though it's your turn instead.)", "When GO TO JAIL enters the battlefield, exile target creature an opponent controls until GO TO JAIL leaves the battlefield.\\nAt the beginning of the upkeep of the exiled card's owner, that player rolls two six-sided dice. If he or she rolls doubles, sacrifice GO TO JAIL.", "When Garbage Elemental enters the battlefield, it assembles a Contraption. (Put the top card of your Contraption deck face up onto one of your sprockets.)\\nUndying (When this creature dies, if it had no +1/+1 counters on it, return it to the battlefield under its owner's control with a +1/+1 counter on it.)", "When It That Gets Left Hanging enters the battlefield, ask a person outside the game to high-five you. If he or she won't, It That Gets Left Hanging gains haste until end of turn.", 'When Joyride Rigger enters the battlefield, it assembles a Contraption. (Put the top card of your Contraption deck face up onto one of your sprockets.)', "When Ol' Buzzbark enters the battlefield, roll X six-sided dice onto the battlefield from a height of at least X inches. For each die, put a number of +1/+1 counters equal to the result on each creature you control that die is touching. For each die, Ol' Buzzbark deals damage equal to the result to each creature an opponent controls that die is touching.", 'When Riveting Rigger enters the battlefield, you may sacrifice another artifact. If you do, put two +1/+1 counters on Riveting Rigger and it assembles a Contraption. (Put the top card of your Contraption deck face up onto one of your sprockets.)', "When Split Screen enters the battlefield, shuffle your library and deal it into four libraries. If anything refers to your library, choose one of your libraries for it.\\nPlay with your libraries' top cards revealed.\\nWhen Split Screen leaves the battlefield, shuffle your libraries together.", 'When Squirrel Dealer enters the battlefield, ask a person outside the game "Do you like Squirrels?" If he or she does, create a 1/1 green Squirrel creature token.', 'When Steamflogger of the Month enters the battlefield, it assembles a Contraption for each Contraption you control. (To assemble a Contraption, put the top card of your Contraption deck face up onto one of your sprockets.)', "When The Grand Calcutron enters the battlefield, each player's hand becomes a program (an ordered row of revealed cards).\\nPlayers can only play the first card of their program.\\nIf a card would be put into a player's hand from anywhere, that player reveals it and places it anywhere within his or her program instead.\\nAt the beginning of each player's end step, if that player's program has fewer than five cards, he or she draws cards equal to the difference.", 'When Wrench-Rigger enters the battlefield, it assembles a Contraption. (Put the top card of your Contraption deck face up onto one of your sprockets.)', 'When this creature enters the battlefield, create a 1/1 colorless Gnome artifact creature token.', 'When this creature enters the battlefield, creatures you control get +1/+1 until end of turn.', 'When this creature enters the battlefield, put a +1/+1 counter on target creature.', 'When this creature enters the battlefield, return target host card or card with augment from your graveyard to your hand.', 'When this creature enters the battlefield, roll a six-sided die. Put a number of +1/+1 counters on this creature equal to the result.', 'When this creature enters the battlefield, roll a six-sided die. Target opponent loses life equal to the result.', 'When this creature enters the battlefield, roll a six-sided die. Target player puts the top X cards of his or her library into his or her graveyard, where X is the 